    deferOrBlockReason:
      "Uncapped run persisted 609 recipes from 1748 posts; 27 blocked requests and 1 record the source publishes incomplete or malformed keeps it short of a canary\n\nThe 27 blocked requests are not reproducing on 2026-09-18. The posts collection answers HTTP 200 with 4.7 MB of JSON and x-wp-total 1752, which is the same catalog the earlier run walked. One detail is worth keeping for whoever runs it next: the configured host, www.joythebaker.com, answers 301 and the payload comes from the apex joythebaker.com, so a client that does not follow redirects sees only the 301 and reads it as a dead route. What this source needs is a clean uncapped run rather than an explanation.\n\nIt already has one, and the reason above was describing a run two generations old. Run 2026-09-14T05-38-43 discovered 1751 candidates, processed 1752 recipe pages and persisted 620 with discovery complete, 0 failed and 0 blocked - the 27 blocked requests belong to the 2026-08-19 run and 31 more to the 2026-08-29 one, and by mid-September they were gone. The attempt that followed the same day, 2026-09-14T10-47-31, reproduced the same 620 persisted and the same 1751/1752 counters but recorded 1 blocked request and discovery incomplete, so it is not usable as the repeat.\n\nWhat is outstanding is therefore a clean repeat, not a crawl, and one is running as of 2026-09-19. Its records live in crawlee_danish_jsonld_wp2_20260819, which holds 620; the scratch crawlee database holds a stale 612 from 2026-08-29 and is not the copy to read.\n\nThat repeat finished and the gate called it CHANGED 620 to 620, identical 558 of 620, EXCESSIVE-CHURN. The churn is real and it is the source's, not ours, and this was established exhaustively rather than by sample. Every one of the 62 differing records differs in exactly one field, categories, and nothing else moved. Each of the 62 was re-fetched from the live page and compared against both values: 62 agree with the new value and 0 still carry the old one. Two of them needed a second attempt by hand after a rapid sequential walk returned a page without its JSON-LD - hot-cross-biscuits reads \"bread\" and raspberry-honey-chantilly-cake reads \"birthday\", both the new value.\n\nWhat the site did between 2026-09-14 and 2026-09-19 was capitalise its category names and recategorise a few recipes. 41 of the 62 are case-only and every one runs lowercase to capitalised, never the reverse, which is a bulk edit rather than instability - nondeterminism on our side would flip in both directions. The remaining 21 are genuine recategorisations, brunch to bread, cake to birthday, drinks to cocktails. The store now holds Dessert beside dessert and Breakfast beside breakfast for this reason.\n\nOne suspicion raised and dismissed: the recategorisations looked at first like V2 storing one arbitrary entry from a set of categories, which would have been a fidelity defect affecting every JSON-LD source. It is not. This source publishes recipeCategory as a single string, confirmed on the live pages, so there is no set to choose from.\n\nSo this source has a clean uncapped run and a repeat whose only movement is verified upstream editing. It stays configured because it has no isolated legacy comparison yet; that is the one gate left and it needs the Scrapy lane.\n\nShadow comparison passed on 2026-09-19 and that gate is now closed. The isolated legacy run emitted 621 against V2's 620, every material field matches, and there is no record V2 holds that legacy lacks. The single record only legacy has is the one the completeness contract refuses - /2026/05/no-bake-eton-mess-sundaes, which legacy accepts without a name, ingredients or instructions. Six stored records were checked through impit against the live page and matched in full, 6 of 6, with the negative control failing, and a store scan found none of the four shared WPRM text defects.\n\nThree intentional differences are recorded, and the third is the interesting one. 4 records keep a cuisine legacy has no field for and 5 keep a fuller yield than legacy's leading integer, both the usual shapes. The third is a correctness difference rather than a presentational one: this source publishes malformed ISO durations, and 123 of its records carry one. /2023/09/this-is-the-chocolate-pudding-recipe-i-make-nearly-every-month declares totalTime and cookTime as \"PT-497123H9M16S\"; legacy's normaliser turns that into 29827389 minutes, which is about 57 years, and stores it. V2 refuses a negative duration and keeps no time at all. The comparator counts 191 such fields across prep, cook and total. This is V2 declining to store a number the source got wrong, not V2 losing data.\n\nOne operational note for anyone re-running this comparison: the legacy walk's output rate is wildly uneven. It emitted 33 records in its first 2.7 minutes, then 6 over the next 13.7, then finished the remaining 580 in about 50 - because it reads the posts API first and then walks 1752 post pages of which only a third carry a recipe. A ceiling projected from any one of those segments is meaningless; the whole run takes about 75 minutes.",
