      "2056 legacy records all present in V2 with 1 newer V2 record; 5 field differences, each a site edit made between the runs or a record the comparator cannot pair",
    latestCanary: "2026-08-20T06-47-19.688Z-attempt-f99d21c1-d3ae-4f80-9d72-133e5b7bfea5",
    deferOrBlockReason:
      "Uncapped run persisted 1499 recipes from 1500 candidates; 1 record the source publishes incomplete or malformed and 1 failed request and discovery that did not complete (unreported) keeps it short of a canary.\n\nA later run cleared that: the 2026-08-29 run stored 2050 recipes from 2052 candidates with discovery complete, nothing failed or blocked, and 2 pages refused as incomplete. The isolated legacy comparison ran 2026-09-12 and legacy finished on its own terms - 21 requests, every one HTTP 200, 2056 records in 51 seconds, because both sides read the same WPRM collection rather than crawling pages.\n\nLegacy holds 6 records V2 does not, and they cannot be checked right now. Within minutes of that run the site began answering this host 403 Forbidden with a 103-byte body - not only on recipe pages but on the homepage and on the wp-json collection both crawlers use. So the six are unverified: the store is 14 days older than legacy, both sides discover through the same endpoint, and recipes published in between would look exactly like this, but that is inference rather than evidence. A same-window re-crawl once the block lifts is what settles it.\n\nThe field differences are small and wait on the same thing: 14 ingredients, 5 yields, 4 instructions, 3 totals, 1 category and 1 image across 2050 records, none of them examined yet because a stale store can produce differences of its own.\n\nThe block is not what it looked like. By 2026-09-13 every request from this host had answered 403 for about 39 hours, including a single request for the homepage, and the same 403 came back through Mullvad exits in Frankfurt and Copenhagen - which read as the site refusing VPN addresses. Every one of those probes used curl, and it is curl the site refuses, by the way it connects. On 2026-09-14 the same host and address fetched the homepage at HTTP 200 with 417937 bytes, and the wprm_recipe collection at 200 with 1387128 bytes, through impit presenting the Chrome TLS handshake; curl got 403 with 103 bytes the same minute. So the refusal keys on the TLS fingerprint of the client, not its address, and no exit rotation would ever have helped. The crawler has used that transport since 2026-09-13.\n\nThat reopens the source. A same-window re-crawl is queued, and the legacy comparison of 2026-09-12 - legacy finished cleanly with 2056 records, before the block began - is recent enough to compare against once the store is fresh.\n\nDone on 2026-09-14. The re-crawl through impit persisted 2057 recipes from 2059 candidates with discovery complete and nothing failed or blocked, refusing 2 as incomplete WPRM records - the first crawl of this source since the block, and proof that the crawler itself gets through rather than only a single probe. Compared with the legacy run of 2026-09-12: no legacy-only records at all, where the stale store had 6, and 1 crawlee-only record, vitello-tonnato, whose own datePublished is 2026-09-12T11:38 UTC - about seven hours after the legacy run finished.\n\nFive field differences remain and each is accounted for. hachis-parmentier has legacy listing okse tyksteg where V2 lists tykkam; the live page says tykkam and no longer mentions okse tyksteg, so the site edited the recipe between the two runs and V2 holds the current text. mørbradbøffer-i-cremet-tomatsauce differs in two instruction steps, checked against the live page: one step reads På samme tallerken in legacy and På samme pande in V2, and the live page says pande, so that is another edit made between the runs; the other is only a space V2 restores after a full stop, where legacy runs peber.Juster together. luksus-tarteletter-med-hoens-i-asparges publishes two recipes at one URL under one title, and the comparator cannot tell them apart, so it pairs each with the other: the 45-minute total appears on both sides, once on each record. Legacy itself cannot run again here, because its Python handshake is exactly what the site refuses.\n\nThe repeat closed the last gate on 2026-09-14: a second uncapped run through impit persisted 2057 recipes from 2059 candidates over 22 requests with discovery complete, no page cap, nothing failed or blocked, and the same 2 refusals, and the repeat gate reported the store 2057 to 2057 with all 2057 identical. This source carries no WPRM inline shortcodes, so the rendering fix of the same day changes nothing in its records - checked by running the old and new extractor over all 2057 stored raw records.",
